'''''Lysimachia monelli''''', the '''blue pimpernel''' or '''garden pimpernel'''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17}}</ref> (formerly known as '''''Anagallis monelli''''') is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Primulaceae]], [[Indigenous (ecology)|native]] to the Mediterranean region (in the [[Iberian Peninsula]], [[Northwest Africa]], [[Corsica]], [[Sicily]] and the [[Balearic Islands]]<ref>{{cite web |title=Lysimachia monelli (L.) U.Manns & Anderb. {{!}} Plants of the World Online {{!}} Kew Science |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:77100405-1 |website=Plants of the World Online |access-date=18 December 2024 |language=en}}</ref>). It is not to be confused with ''[[Lysimachia foemina]]'', which has very similar blue flowers, but broader leaves and can be found also in colder climates. In a comparison of [[DNA sequence]]s, ''L. monelli'' was shown to be most closely [[Phylogenetics|related]] to ''L. foemina''.<ref name="manns2007">{{cite journal | last = Manns | first = Ulrika |author2=Anderberg, AA  | title = Relationships of ''Anagallis foemina'' and ''Anagallis arvensis'' (Primulaceae): New insights inferred from DNA sequence data | journal = Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution | volume = 45 | issue = 3 | pages = 971–980 | date = Dec 2007 | doi = 10.1016/j.ympev.2007.07.022 | pmid=17869544}}</ref> The latter had been thought by many to be closest to ''[[Lysimachia arvensis|L. arvensis]]'', and some authors had even included ''L. foemina'' as a [[subspecies]] of ''L. arvensis''.{{citation needed|date=August 2013}} The three species were among several transferred from ''[[Anagallis]]'' to ''[[Lysimachia]]'' in a 2009 paper.<ref>{{cite journal|title= New combinations and names in ''Lysimachia'' (Primulaceae) for species of ''Anagallis'', ''Pelletiera'' and ''Trientalis'' |author=Manns, Ulrika |author2=Anderberg, Arne A.|date=July 2009|journal=Willdenowia|pages=49–54|volume=39|issue=1|jstor=20699148|doi=10.3372/wi.39.39103|doi-access=free}}</ref>

''Lysimachia monelli'' is a low-growing [[perennial plant|perennial]] with trailing stems. Wild specimens have blue or orange coloured flowers and are not [[Sympatry|sympatric]] with the blue-flowered plants growing natively in southern Spain and the orange in Morocco and southern Italy. A red variant was also developed by [[Plant breeding|breeding]] at the [[University of New Hampshire]].  The orange-coloured flowers have a higher concentration of [[pelargonidin]] pigment, while blue flowers have a higher concentration of [[malvidin]].  The red-coloured flowers are due to the relative concentrations of [[delphinidin]] and malvidin pigments.  In addition to the blue, orange and red forms, a white form of the flower also exists.<ref>{{cite journal | last = Quintana | first = Andrea | author2 = Freyre, Rosanna | author3 = Davis, Thomas M. | author4 = Griesbach, Robert J. | title = Genetic Studies of Flower Color in ''Anagallis monelli'' L. | journal = HortScience | volume = 43 | issue = 6 | pages = 1680–1685 | date = 2008 | doi = 10.21273/hortsci.43.6.1680 | hdl = 10113/21705| doi-access = free }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal | last = Quintana | first = Andrea |author2=Albrechtova, Jana |author3=Davis, Tom |author4=Griesbach, Robert J. |author5=Freyre, Rosanna | title = Genetics, anatomy and biochemistry of flower color in ''Anagallis monelli'' (L.) 'Pimpernel' | journal = HortScience | volume = 40 | issue = 4 | pages = 1002–1003 | date = July 2005 | doi = 10.21273/hortsci.40.4.1002e| doi-access = free }}</ref>

Although this is a perennial, in cultivation in [[temperateness|temperate]] regions this plant is often grown as an [[annual plant|annual]]. As ''Anagallis monellii'' it has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web |title=''Anagallis monellii'' blue pimpernel |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/67647/anagallis-monellii/details |date=2021 |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=11 May 2021 }}</ref>

